.TH ai-chat 3
.XI ai-chat - "Open the AI coding assistant chat"
.XI ai-send - "Send the chat question to the AI"
.XI ai-ask - "Ask the AI a one line question"
.XI ai-code-action - "Ask the AI about the region or buffer"
.XI ai-insert-block - "Insert a code block from the AI chat"
.XI ai-new - "Start a new AI chat"
.XI ai-cancel - "Cancel the AI reply"
.XI ai-set-model - "Choose the AI model"
.SH NAME
.Me "|ai-chat"
ai-chat \- Open the AI coding assistant chat
.br
.Me "|ai-send"
ai-send \- Send the chat question to the AI
.br
.Me "|ai-ask"
ai-ask \- Ask the AI a one line question
.br
.Me "|ai-code-action"
ai-code-action \- Ask the AI about the region or buffer
.br
.Me "|ai-insert-block"
ai-insert-block \- Insert a code block from the AI chat
.br
.Me "|ai-new"
ai-new \- Start a new AI chat
.br
.Me "|ai-cancel"
ai-cancel \- Cancel the AI reply
.br
.Me "|ai-set-model"
ai-set-model \- Choose the AI model
.Me $a
.SH SYNOPSIS
.na
\fBai-chat\fR
.br
\fBai-send\fR
.br
\fBai-ask\fR "\fIquestion\fR"
.br
\fBai-code-action\fR "\fIaction\fR|\fIquestion\fR"
.br
\fBai-insert-block\fR [\|\fCr\fR|\fCi\fR|\fCc\fR\|]
.br
\fBai-new\fR
.br
\fBai-cancel\fR
.br
\fBai-set-model\fR "\fImodel\fR"
.ad
.SH DESCRIPTION
These commands hold a conversation about code with an AI model through an OpenAI
compatible chat completions API, either OpenAI itself or a local server such as
Ollama, LM Studio or llama.cpp.
.PP
The conversation is the text of the \fC*ai-chat*\fR buffer. Each part starts
with a line holding just \fC## You\fR, \fC## AI\fR or \fC## System\fR, anything
before the first of these is ignored. The whole chat is sent with every
question, so earlier questions and answers may be edited or deleted to change
what the model sees, and the buffer may be saved to a file and the chat carried
on later from any buffer holding these lines. A \fC## System\fR part replaces the
system prompt given by \fB%ai-system-prompt\fR. The buffer uses the markdown
major mode so the code blocks in a reply are hilighted.
.PP
.B ai-chat
shows the \fC*ai-chat*\fR buffer in a popup window and moves to its end, ready
for a question to be written under the last \fC## You\fR line.
.PP
.B ai-send
sends the chat in the current buffer, the last part of which must be a
\fC## You\fR question. The reply is added under a new \fC## AI\fR line, followed
by an empty \fC## You\fR for the next question. Only one request may run at a
time. In the \fC*ai-chat*\fR buffer the following keys are bound:
.IP "\fC\*cc \*cc\fR"
.B ai-send
.IP "\fC\*cc \*ck\fR"
.B ai-cancel
.IP "\fC\*cc i\fR"
.B ai-insert-block
.IP "\fC\*cc n\fR"
.B ai-new
.IP "\fC\*cc m\fR"
.B ai-set-model
.LP
.B ai-ask
prompts for a one line question, adds it to the chat and sends it, leaving the
cursor where it was while the reply appears in the chat window.
.PP
.B ai-code-action
sends code from the current buffer with an instruction. The code is the active
region, or the whole buffer when there is none, and goes into the chat as a
fenced block tagged with the buffer's major mode, after a line naming the buffer
and the lines sent. The user is prompted for one of the actions listed in
\fB%ai-actions\fR, with completion, or may type any other question instead. The
actions are:
.IP \fBexplain\fR
Explain what the code does.
.IP \fBreview\fR
List bugs, edge cases and readability problems, most serious first.
.IP \fBfix\fR
Reply with the corrected code and a list of the changes.
.IP \fBdocument\fR
Reply with the code with documentation comments added.
.IP \fBtests\fR
Write unit tests for the code.
.IP \fBsimplify\fR
Reply with simpler code that behaves the same.
.LP
The text of action \fIname\fR is held in \fB%ai-action-\fIname\fR, so an action
may be changed, or a new one added to \fB%ai-actions\fR with its own variable.
.PP
.B ai-insert-block
copies a fenced code block from the chat. In the chat it uses the block holding
the cursor, or the last block before it; from any other buffer it uses the last
block in \fC*ai-chat*\fR. The user is then asked whether to replace the region
last sent by
.B ai-code-action
with it (\fCr\fR, only offered when a region was sent), to insert it at the
cursor of that buffer (\fCi\fR) or just to copy it to the kill buffer and the
system clipboard (\fCc\fR). The choice may instead be given as an argument. A
replacement can be repeated with a later block, as the region is moved to cover
the new code.
.PP
.B ai-new
clears the chat, asking first if it holds any replies.
.PP
.B ai-cancel
stops a reply that is streaming in, keeping what has arrived so far.
.PP
.B ai-set-model
fetches the list of models from the server and prompts for one, with
completion. If the list cannot be fetched any name may be typed. The model is
saved in the registry, so it is remembered by later sessions; when no model has
been set the first request prompts for one.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The following variables may be set in the user's \fIuser\fR.emf setup file:
.IP \fB%ai-base-url\fR
The API address, \fChttps://api.openai.com/v1\fR by default.
.IP \fB%ai-api-key\fR
The API key. When empty the \fCOPENAI_API_KEY\fR environment variable is used,
which avoids writing the key into a setup file. A local server usually needs no
key.
.IP \fB%ai-model\fR
The model, normally set with
.BR ai-set-model .
.IP \fB%ai-transport\fR
\fCcurl\fR to stream the reply through \fCcurl\fR, \fChttp\fR to use the built in
HTTP client, or empty (the default) to use \fCcurl\fR when it is found on the
path. \fB%ai-curl\fR gives the \fCcurl\fR program, \fCcurl\fR by default.
.IP \fB%ai-system-prompt\fR
The instructions sent before the chat.
.IP \fB%ai-wrap\fR
When 1 (the default) the long prose lines of a reply are split at the buffer's
fill column once it has arrived, leaving code blocks, tables and headings alone.
Set to 0 to keep the reply as sent.
.LP
For example, to use a coding model served by Ollama on the local machine:
.Me $emf-scheme
.CS
set-variable %ai-base-url "http://localhost:11434/v1"
set-variable %ai-model "qwen2.5-coder"
.CE 0
.Me $ehf-scheme
.SH NOTES
These are macros defined in \fCaichat.emf\fR.
.PP
Nothing is sent unless one of the commands above is run, but everything in the
chat, including any code added to it, is then sent to the server.
.PP
With \fCcurl\fR the reply streams into the chat as it is written and editing can
carry on meanwhile. The API key is passed in a header file readable only by the
user, which is deleted as soon as the reply starts. The built in HTTP client
waits for the whole reply, during which the editor does not respond; it keeps
the key in memory, removing flag \fCd\fR from \fB%http-flags\fR for the request
so the key is not logged in \fC*http-console*\fR. HTTPS through the built in
client needs a version of \*(mS built with OpenSSL.
.PP
A reply line cannot grow past the 65000 character line limit, so a longer one is
broken. A request is built as UTF-8, which needs a version of \*(mS with Unicode
support for characters outside ASCII.
